<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0

  Privacy manifest. Required by App Store Connect: without one, uploads draw ITMS-91053 warnings and
  the privacy report Apple generates for the listing cannot be assembled.

  All four keys are declared explicitly, including the empty ones. An absent key and an empty array
  mean the same thing to Apple's tooling but not to a reader — "we collect nothing" and "nobody filled
  this in" would otherwise be the same file — and this app genuinely collects nothing.

    NSPrivacyTracking            false  no tracking of any kind.
    NSPrivacyTrackingDomains     empty  nothing to list. There are no third-party SDKs at all;
                                        Package.swift declares no .package(url:) dependencies, so
                                        there are no vendored manifests to reconcile with this one.
    NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes  empty  the food journal never leaves the device. It is a JSON file in
                                        Application Support written by App/Model/Journal.swift, and the
                                        only network calls are GET /ingredients/search and
                                        POST /nutrition, which send a food name, a weight and a cooking
                                        method — no identifier, no account, no device data.
    NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes    empty  see below.

  On the last one, because an empty array here is a claim and not a default: the required-reason API
  categories are file timestamp, system boot time, disk space, active keyboard and user defaults.
  Journal.swift is the only file touching the filesystem and it uses exactly four calls —
  FileManager.urls(for:in:), createDirectory, removeItem and moveItem. None of those is a
  required-reason API: it never reads a creation or modification date, never inspects free space, and
  UserDefaults appears nowhere in the target. Declaring a category anyway — C617.1 for timestamps, or
  E174.1 for disk space — would be over-declaring, which misdescribes the app just as surely as
  under-declaring does.

  Recheck this file if Journal.swift ever sorts by file date, reports free space, or if UserDefaults
  arrives with a settings screen.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>

    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>

    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>

    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array/>
</dict>
</plist>
